# Skip lists

A sorted linked list with express lanes. Each node gets a random height (level i with probability 1/2^i). Search starts at the top level and drops down when the next node would overshoot.

Expected O(log n) search, insert and delete, with no rotations or rebalancing, which makes concurrent versions much easier than for balanced trees.

Space: about 2n pointers on average with p = 1/2 (fewer with p = 1/4).

Used in Redis sorted sets, LevelDB/RocksDB memtables and Java's ConcurrentSkipListMap.

Compared with a red-black tree: similar performance, simpler code, but only probabilistic guarantees and worse cache locality.

My implementation bug: I generated the level once per list instead of once per node, so every node had the same height and the structure was just several copies of a linked list.
